WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:04.490
Have you ever heard of the central dogma of biology?

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.240
It's the concept that genetic information stored as DNA gets turned into 

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.920
RNA, a process called transcription,

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:13.750
which gets turned into protein.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.800
The idea that the flow of information is unidirectional was put forth by 

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.430
Francis Crick in 1958.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.070
The very word, dogma, means that this is the truth,

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:25.840
a fact,

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.960
so can you imagine challenging this established truth? Well, in the 

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:31.420
1970s,

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.950
scientists found an exception to the central dogma.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.600
These scientists were studying viruses,

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:42.860
and they realized that some viruses, called retroviruses, use RNA, 

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:46.510
and not DNA, to store their genetic information.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.740
This is different from most other organisms.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:51.960
Why was this interesting to scientists,

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.530
and how did it lead to their questioning of the central dogma?

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:56.940
To answer this,

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:58.990
let's first talk about retroviruses.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:05.030
Retroviruses are small particles that consist of genetic information and 

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.490
a few proteins inside a capsule.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.420
These viruses need to be inside the cell of another host organism 

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:13.600
to replicate.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:14.760
Once inside,

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:19.180
viruses copy their genetic material to form many more viruses.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.860
Scientists knew how DNA was copied to make more DNA.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:27.350
They also knew how DNA was copied to make RNA,

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.850
but retroviruses posed a puzzle.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:34.430
How can the RNA in these viruses be copied?

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:36.600
Back in the early 1970s,

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:41.970
David Baltimore and Howard Temin hypothesized that retroviruses might 

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:44.330
have a special kind of enzyme,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.420
a polymerase, that could turn their RNA into DNA.

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.860
So they went on a search for this enzyme.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:52.130
First,

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:57.090
the scientists isolated a candidate enzyme that could use an RNA template 

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:58.150
and make a product.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.060
But what was the product?

00:02:00.598 --> 00:02:03.478
Was it copies of DNA or of RNA?

00:02:04.188 --> 00:02:05.478
To answer this question,

00:02:05.658 --> 00:02:10.608
they combined the purified enzyme with the RNA template and different kinds of 

00:02:10.608 --> 00:02:13.818
nucleotides to be incorporated into the new product.

00:02:14.698 --> 00:02:19.238
If the nucleotides given were ribonucleotides, the building blocks of 

00:02:19.348 --> 00:02:19.818
RNA,

00:02:20.118 --> 00:02:21.558
no product was produced.

00:02:22.128 --> 00:02:26.988
If instead, they gave deoxyribonucleotides, the building blocks 

00:02:26.998 --> 00:02:27.838
of DNA,

00:02:28.188 --> 00:02:29.618
they could get a product.

00:02:30.008 --> 00:02:34.848
This strongly suggested that the viral polymerase was converting the RNA 

00:02:34.848 --> 00:02:39.378
template into a DNA product, and not an RNA product. 

00:02:40.608 --> 00:02:45.418
Information was flowing in the opposite direction that Francis Crick had proposed 

00:02:45.578 --> 00:02:46.828
in the central dogma.

00:02:47.688 --> 00:02:52.008
This enzyme was later named reverse transcriptase because it does 

00:02:52.008 --> 00:02:53.948
transcription, but backwards.

00:02:53.958 --> 00:02:58.818
This exciting new discovery earned Baltimore and Temin the Nobel 

00:02:58.818 --> 00:02:59.948
Prize in 1975.

00:03:01.608 --> 00:03:06.358
Baltimore and Temin's finding had a major impact on modern medicine and 

00:03:06.358 --> 00:03:10.098
biotechnology. Before the modern genomic era,

00:03:10.308 --> 00:03:14.998
the identification of most human genes involved copying human RNA 

00:03:15.358 --> 00:03:19.318
into DNA by reverse transcriptase, to be used for further studies.

00:03:19.998 --> 00:03:24.588
Another important example is HIV, the retrovirus that causes 

00:03:24.598 --> 00:03:28.158
AIDS and effects 35 million people worldwide.

00:03:29.158 --> 00:03:34.128
HIV uses reverse transcriptase to replicate and spread. Thanks 

00:03:34.128 --> 00:03:39.078
to Baltimore and Temin's discovery, physicians now use medicines that block 

00:03:39.078 --> 00:03:42.358
reverse transcriptase to treat patients with HIV,

00:03:42.818 --> 00:03:45.578
and all of this from questioning the dogma.

